{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:cb8dd713a7319d6d10525e59829983693f87bb36eafe08bd56ed2d6f67144644","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.34},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"CONJUNCT A (GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE avg >=2.50%/yr, 2027-2036) = 0.54. This is where the effort genuinely learned something across waves, and the credit is unambiguous: the Resolution AUDITOR found that the published GDP-weighted OECD-38 aggregate CONTAINS TURKIYE, and the FERMI panelist converted it into a member-by-member PPP-weighted build the other four never attempted — US 0.395 at 2.45, euro 0.235 at 2.05, Japan 0.090 at 1.80, Turkiye 0.040 at 9.0, UK 0.050 at 2.50, Korea 0.035 at 2.00, Mexico 0.035 at 3.80, Canada 0.030 at 2.30, Australia 0.020 at 2.60, rest 0.070 at 2.60 → 2.603% inclusive, 2.337% ex-Turkiye, 2.28% ex-Turkiye-and-Mexico. The two high-inflation members are worth +0.32pp and they straddle the threshold. I therefore HEAVILY DOWN-WEIGHT the Reference-Class SCOUT (0.33, read off historical OECD core aggregates whose composition he never reconciled to the current basket) and the Mechanism MODELER (0.40, built on US 0.45 / euro 0.30 / Japan 0.09 / rest 0.16 weights that sum to 1.00 only by excluding Turkiye and Mexico from the universe the question names). With sigma ~0.28 on a 10-yr average: P(A|inclusive) = 0.64, P(A|ex-Turkiye) = 0.27, resolver weights 0.65/0.35 → 0.515. I do NOT take Fermi's round-up to 0.57: his +5-8pp for core CPI vs core PCE partly double-counts, since his own US central of 2.45 is already closer to a CPI-basis than a PCE-basis number. Against that, the RED-TEAM lands a genuine coupling hit that pushes DOWN and that nobody else priced: fiscal stress is expressed in the PRICE OF DURATION or in the PRICE LEVEL, and these are substitutes at the margin — if the sovereign clears its rollover at a high real yield (my CQ4 P50 is 2.15%, above four of five panelists) the inflation tax is not required. I apply that as a partial, not full, discount — 1945-1980 and the 1970s ran both at once — netting to P(A) = 0.54.\nCONJUNCT B (China headline CPI avg <1.50%/yr, 2027-2036) = 0.68. Panel 0.66/0.72/0.62/0.65/0.70. The decisive arithmetic is Fermi's: to REACH a 1.50% ten-year mean from a 0.0-0.3% run rate, China needs ~1.8-2.0%/yr over 2029-2036 — a genuine reflation, not drift. Supporting: NBS does not back-revise; the series is widely assessed as smoothed and a lower-variance series near a 0.5-1.0% trend is mechanically likelier to stay inside a band; 2A locks -60M population 2026-2035 and worker/retiree 4.4->2.8; Japan 1995-2015 averaged ~0.0-0.3% over twenty years. The live reflation channel the red-team pressed is inside the locked block, not exogenous — pension/social-security subsidies at 2.9T RMB, 10.1% of fiscal expenditure, heading past 15%, a transfer to high-MPC retirees that could release the precautionary saving holding prices down — plus the 2026 basket rebase (pork weight alone has swung headline 0.5-1.0pp) and RMB pass-through on food imports. 0.68.\nCORRELATION: two offsetting channels. Positive: both legs are the same OECD-non-tradable-bottleneck / China-tradable-glut bifurcation. Negative and mechanical: Chinese overcapacity exports goods disinflation into OECD core goods (~25-30% of the core basket; a 1pp lower China CPI is worth 0.05-0.10pp lower OECD core), but OECD core is service-heavy so the drag is weak. Net rho = -0.10.\nJOINT = 0.54 x 0.68 − 0.10 x sqrt(0.54x0.46x0.68x0.32) = 0.367 − 0.10 x 0.2324 = 0.344 -> 34. I land at the Wave-2 level, not the Wave-1 centre of 27, and the entire move is conjunct A's resolving-basket composition — an accounting fact, not a macro view.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:53.851Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"G7 GDP-weighted 10y sovereign real yield (10y nominal benchmark minus 10y inflation swap / breakeven, computed on the same seven benchmarks) averages between 1.70% and 2.60% in EVERY one of 2030, 2031 and 2032, with no G7 sovereign having adopted explicit yield-curve control, a statutory captive-holding mandate for banks or insurers, or a central-bank 'market functioning' purchase programme above 3% of GDP.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This is the signpost most specific to ME, because my distinguishing claim is not the level but the BIMODALITY: a three-year run inside a 90bp band with the policy branch unfired says the distribution is unimodal and my 54% branch-switch variance attribution is wrong. CQ4 collapses to roughly P10 1.60 / P50 2.20 / P90 2.90 (narrower, not shifted), the variance attribution reverts to premium-dominant (~50%) as the Wave-1 panel had it, CQ5 falls from 28 to ~22 because the bimodal-switch channel that fattens the rate-vol distribution disappears, and ~4pp moves from CQ6-C into B.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The New York Fed HLW r* estimate for the United States (or the Richmond Fed Lubik-Matthes companion) is published at or below 1.0% for both the 2028 and 2029 vintages with no upward revision to the 2024-27 history, WHILE the US 10y TIPS real yield (FRED DFII10) averages below 1.70% over any rolling twelve months in the absence of any announced YCC, duration-shortening or captive-buyer mandate.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills my partial concession to the red-team that the filter is revising into a regime break, and vindicates the Wave-1 low-and-flat r* I moved away from. r* falls from 1.05% to ~0.80%, CQ4 P50 drops ~30bp to ~1.85 with P90 to ~2.80, CQ6-D rises from 13 to ~18 and CQ6-A falls ~2pp (cheap duration signals abundant saving, which is the demand-shortfall regime), and my CQ2 conjunct A rises ~4pp as the duration-price-versus-price-level substitution reverses.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Japan's 10y JGB nominal yield stays below 2.25% AND Germany's 10y Bund below 3.00% on a twelve-month average, with the Bank of Japan holding a policy rate at or below 1.0%, in both 2031 and 2032.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the Japan/euro convergence-upward component worth ~+25-35bp of my CQ4 recomposition — the component the Mechanism Modeler described in words and then neutralized, and that I restored. CQ4 P50 falls ~30bp to ~1.85 and the whole distribution shifts down without changing shape; CQ5 falls ~3pp through the MOVE level channel; CQ3 and CQ6 are essentially unchanged, because this is a price signpost, not a quantity one. It would also mean my 50/50 PPP-versus-market-FX weighting split mattered less than the component levels.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Operational data-centre electricity across OECD-38 + China (strict operational definition) reaches 1,000 TWh/yr or more in 2036 WHILE cumulative announced-buildout impairments and cancellations exceed 20% of announced hyperscaler capex — i.e. the quantity arrives AND the write-downs arrive together.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2036\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CONFIRMS the red-team's reversal that I adopted against the Wave-1 unanimous direction — that AI-Q3 is a quantity question gated by interconnection queues, transformers and licensed electricians rather than a financing question gated by the term premium, so a higher cost of capital changes who owns and who writes down the asset, not the megawatts. The converse observation (Q3 below 900 TWh with impairments above 20%) would falsify it and restore the Wave-1 coupling direction, moving my coupling note's Q3 call from flat-to-up back to down and raising the weight I place on the loanable-funds channel in r* from +5bp toward +15bp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >=25% occurs and G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yields fall by >=100bp from the equity peak date to the trough date — the bond hedge works emphatically, not marginally — while the trailing MOVE average since Jan 2027 is still below 100.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Consumes CQ5's most likely qualifying episode with a non-qualifying case and confirms the Wave-1 disinflationary-bust premise against the red-team's credit-event argument that I partly accepted. CQ5 falls from 28 to ~13 (short remaining window, both conjuncts weakened simultaneously). It also implies abundant precautionary saving, so CQ4 P50 falls ~30bp and my repression branch probability falls from 0.38 toward 0.28 (a sovereign that still rallies in stress needs no policy backstop); CQ6-D rises from 13 to ~18 at B's and C's expense.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 3 reconciler seat, effort 'rstar'."}}